/* Electric Circuits cards. Card format: see AGENTS.md. */
(function(){
const R = String.raw;
addCards('ckt', [

/* ---------- Network elements ---------- */
{id:'srctrans',t:'elem',u:'src',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Source transformation',sheet:R`V_s\text{ in series with }R\ \equiv\ \frac{V_s}{R}\text{ in parallel with }R`,
 q:R`Complete the source transformation.`,f:R`V_s\text{ in series with }R\ \equiv\ \boxed{\;?\;}\text{ in parallel with }R`,
 o:[R`\frac{V_s}{R}`,R`V_sR`,R`\frac{R}{V_s}`,R`V_s`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Both have the same open-circuit voltage \(V_s\) and the same short-circuit current \(V_s/R\). The resistance stays the same.`},
{id:'idealsrc',t:'elem',u:'src',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'Ideal sources',sheet:R`\text{ideal } V\text{-source: } R_{int}=0;\ \text{ideal } I\text{-source: } R_{int}=\infty`,
 q:R`Which statements about ideal sources are true?`,
 o:[R`An ideal voltage source has zero internal resistance`,R`An ideal current source has infinite internal resistance`,R`Two ideal voltage sources of different values cannot be connected in parallel`,R`An ideal current source may be left open-circuited`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Its terminal voltage does not depend on the current.`,R`Its current does not depend on the terminal voltage.`,R`KVL round the loop would fail.`,R`KCL fails: the current has nowhere to go. (Short-circuiting it is fine.)`],
 why:R`Short a current source or open a voltage source, never the other way round.`},
{id:'srcnat',t:'elem',u:'src',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Source transformation',sheet:R`I_L=I_s\frac{R_s}{R_s+R_L}`,
 q:R`A 10 A current source in parallel with 5 Ω feeds a 15 Ω load. Find the load current in A.`,
 n:{v:2.5},
 why:R`Current divider: \(10\times\frac{5}{5+15}=2.5\) A. Equivalently a 50 V source behind 5 Ω drives 20 Ω.`},
{id:'psc',t:'elem',u:'src',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Passive sign convention',sheet:R`\text{current enters the + terminal}\Rightarrow p=vi\text{ is absorbed}`,
 q:R`For an element with voltage v and current i, when does \(p=vi>0\) mean the element absorbs power?`,
 o:[R`When the current enters the element at its + terminal`,R`When the current leaves the element at its + terminal`,R`Only for resistors`,R`Only when v and i are both positive`],a:0,
 why:R`That is the passive sign convention. A source delivering power has current leaving its + terminal, so its \(vi\) (by this convention) is negative.`},
{id:'deptypes',t:'elem',u:'dep',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Dependent source types',sheet:R`\text{VCVS }(\mu),\ \text{CCVS }(r\ [\Omega]),\ \text{VCCS }(g\ [S]),\ \text{CCCS }(\beta)`,
 q:R`The gain of a current-controlled voltage source (CCVS) has the unit:`,
 o:[R`Ohm`,R`Siemens`,R`None (dimensionless)`,R`Volt`],a:0,
 why:R`Output volts per controlling ampere is a transresistance. A VCCS gain is in siemens; VCVS and CCCS gains are dimensionless.`},
{id:'deponly',t:'elem',u:'dep',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Thevenin equivalent with only dependent sources',sheet:R`\text{no independent source}\Rightarrow V_{th}=0,\ R_{th}=\frac{V_{test}}{I_{test}}`,
 q:R`A network contains resistors and dependent sources but no independent source. Its Thevenin equivalent is:`,
 o:[R`A resistance only (\(V_{th}=0\))`,R`A voltage source only`,R`Undefined`,R`Always an open circuit`],a:0,
 why:R`Nothing drives the circuit, so the open-circuit voltage is zero. Find \(R_{th}\) by applying a test source and computing \(V_{test}/I_{test}\).`},
{id:'depnat',t:'elem',u:'dep',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Circuit with a dependent source',sheet:R`\text{KVL with the controlled voltage written in terms of the control variable}`,
 q:R`A single loop has a 10 V source, a 2 Ω resistor and a CCVS of value \(3i\) (i = loop current) that opposes the 10 V source. Find i in A.`,
 n:{v:2},
 why:R`KVL: \(10=2i+3i\), so \(i=2\) A. The CCVS acts like a 3 Ω resistor here.`},
{id:'deprth',t:'elem',u:'dep',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Thevenin equivalent with only dependent sources',sheet:R`R_{th}=\frac{V_{test}}{I_{test}}`,
 q:R`Looking into terminals a–b there is a 4 Ω resistor in series with a CCVS of value \(5i\), where i is the current entering terminal a. The CCVS polarity opposes the resistor drop (its voltage is \(-5i\) along the direction of i). Find \(R_{th}\) in Ω.`,
 n:{v:-1},
 why:R`\(V=4i-5i=-i\), so \(R_{th}=-1\) Ω. Dependent sources can make the equivalent resistance negative.`},
{id:'capenergy',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Energy stored in L and C',sheet:R`W_C=\tfrac12CV^2,\ \ W_L=\tfrac12LI^2`,
 q:R`Complete the energy stored in a capacitor.`,f:R`W_C=\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`\tfrac12CV^2`,R`CV^2`,R`\tfrac12CV`,R`\tfrac12C^2V`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(W=\int vi\,dt=\int Cv\,dv=\frac12CV^2\). The inductor version is \(\frac12LI^2\).`},
{id:'contvar',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Continuity of capacitor voltage and inductor current',sheet:R`v_C(0^+)=v_C(0^-),\ \ i_L(0^+)=i_L(0^-)\ \text{(finite sources)}`,
 q:R`With finite sources, which quantities cannot change instantly at a switching instant?`,
 o:[R`Capacitor voltage and inductor current`,R`Capacitor current and inductor voltage`,R`Capacitor voltage and inductor voltage`,R`All voltages and currents`],a:0,
 why:R`\(i_C=C\,dv/dt\) and \(v_L=L\,di/dt\): a jump would need infinite current or voltage. Capacitor current and inductor voltage can jump.`},
{id:'capseries',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Series and parallel L and C',sheet:R`C_{series}=\frac{C_1C_2}{C_1+C_2},\ \ L_{parallel}=\frac{L_1L_2}{L_1+L_2}\ (\text{no coupling})`,
 q:R`Capacitors of 6 µF and 3 µF are connected in series. Find the equivalent capacitance in µF.`,
 n:{v:2},
 why:R`\(\frac{6\times3}{6+3}=2\) µF. Capacitors combine like resistors in the opposite way (series behaves like parallel resistors).`},
{id:'indpar',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Series and parallel L and C',sheet:R`L_{parallel}=\frac{L_1L_2}{L_1+L_2}\ (\text{no coupling})`,
 q:R`Uncoupled inductors of 4 H and 12 H are connected in parallel. Find the equivalent inductance in H.`,
 n:{v:3},
 why:R`\(\frac{4\times12}{16}=3\) H. Uncoupled inductors combine like resistors.`},
{id:'dcLC',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'L and C at DC steady state',sheet:R`\text{DC steady state: } C\to\text{open},\ L\to\text{short}`,
 q:R`In DC steady state, an inductor and a capacitor behave as:`,
 o:[R`Short circuit and open circuit`,R`Open circuit and short circuit`,R`Both short circuits`,R`Both open circuits`],a:0,
 why:R`With nothing changing, \(v_L=L\,di/dt=0\) and \(i_C=C\,dv/dt=0\).`},
{id:'indenergy',t:'elem',u:'rlc',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Energy stored in L and C',sheet:R`W_L=\tfrac12LI^2`,
 q:R`A 2 H inductor carries 3 A. Find the stored energy in J.`,
 n:{v:9},
 why:R`\(\frac12\times2\times9=9\) J.`},
{id:'mutM',t:'elem',u:'mut',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Coupling coefficient',sheet:R`M=k\sqrt{L_1L_2},\ \ 0\le k\le1`,
 q:R`Complete the mutual inductance of two coupled coils.`,f:R`M=k\,\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`\sqrt{L_1L_2}`,R`L_1L_2`,R`\frac{L_1+L_2}{2}`,R`\sqrt{L_1+L_2}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`k = 1 means all the flux of one coil links the other (perfect coupling).`},
{id:'aidnat',t:'elem',u:'mut',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Series-aiding and series-opposing coils',sheet:R`L_{eq}=L_1+L_2\pm2M`,
 q:R`Coils of 4 H and 9 H with k = 0.5 are connected in series aiding. Find the total inductance in H.`,
 n:{v:19},
 why:R`\(M=0.5\sqrt{36}=3\) H. Aiding: \(4+9+2(3)=19\) H; opposing would give 7 H.`},
{id:'Mfromtwo',t:'elem',u:'mut',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Series-aiding and series-opposing coils',sheet:R`L_{aid}-L_{opp}=4M`,
 q:R`Two coupled coils measure 20 mH in series aiding and 8 mH in series opposing. Find M in mH.`,
 n:{v:3},
 why:R`\((L_1+L_2+2M)-(L_1+L_2-2M)=4M=12\), so \(M=3\) mH.`},
{id:'dotconv',t:'elem',u:'mut',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Dot convention',sheet:R`i_1\text{ entering a dot}\Rightarrow\text{induced } M\frac{di_1}{dt}\text{ is + at the dot of coil 2}`,
 q:R`Current \(i_1\) enters the dotted terminal of coil 1. The voltage \(M\,di_1/dt\) induced in coil 2 is:`,
 o:[R`Positive at the dotted terminal of coil 2`,R`Negative at the dotted terminal of coil 2`,R`Always zero when coil 2 is open`,R`Independent of the dots`],a:0,
 why:R`Dots mark terminals with the same polarity of induced voltage. If the current leaves the dot, the sign flips.`},
{id:'kmax',t:'elem',u:'mut',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Coupling coefficient',sheet:R`M\le\sqrt{L_1L_2}`,
 q:R`Two coils have \(L_1=4\) H and \(L_2=1\) H. Which value of M is impossible?`,
 o:[R`3 H`,R`2 H`,R`1 H`,R`0.5 H`],a:0,
 why:R`\(M_{max}=\sqrt{4\times1}=2\) H (k = 1). 3 H would need k = 1.5.`},

/* ---------- Network solution methods ---------- */
{id:'vdiv',t:'ana',u:'kcl',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Voltage divider',sheet:R`V_1=V\frac{R_1}{R_1+R_2}`,
 q:R`Complete the voltage across \(R_1\) in a series pair.`,f:R`V_1=V\cdot\frac{\boxed{\;?\;}}{R_1+R_2}`,
 o:[R`R_1`,R`R_2`,R`R_1R_2`,R`1`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`The larger resistor takes the larger share of the voltage. The current divider is the opposite.`},
{id:'cdiv',t:'ana',u:'kcl',lv:1,k:'bug',name:'Current divider',sheet:R`I_1=I\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}`,
 q:R`Two resistors in parallel share a current I. Find the mistake.`,f:R`I_1=I\cdot\frac{R_1}{R_1+R_2}`,
 o:[R`The numerator should be \(R_2\), the other resistor`,R`The denominator should be \(R_1R_2\)`,R`It should be \(I\cdot R_1/R_2\)`,R`Nothing, it is correct`],a:0,
 why:R`More current takes the easier path, so the smaller resistor carries more. With conductances: \(I_1=I\frac{G_1}{G_1+G_2}\).`},
{id:'kclnat',t:'ana',u:'kcl',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Kirchhoff’s current law',sheet:R`\sum i_{in}=\sum i_{out}`,
 q:R`Currents of 3 A and 5 A enter a node. Currents of 2 A and i leave it. Find i in A.`,
 n:{v:6},
 why:R`\(3+5=2+i\Rightarrow i=6\) A. KCL is conservation of charge.`},
{id:'reqnat',t:'ana',u:'kcl',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Series–parallel reduction',sheet:R`R_s=R_1+R_2,\ \ R_p=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}`,
 q:R`A 6 Ω resistor in parallel with 3 Ω is connected in series with 4 Ω. Find the total resistance in Ω.`,
 n:{v:6},
 why:R`\(6\parallel3=2\) Ω, plus 4 Ω gives 6 Ω.`},
{id:'kirch',t:'ana',u:'kcl',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'Kirchhoff’s laws',sheet:R`\text{KCL: charge conservation};\ \text{KVL: energy conservation (conservative field)}`,
 q:R`Which statements are true for lumped circuits?`,
 o:[R`KCL follows from conservation of charge`,R`KVL follows from conservation of energy`,R`KCL and KVL hold for nonlinear and time-varying elements too`,R`KVL holds only for resistive circuits`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Charge cannot pile up at a node.`,R`The work done round a closed loop is zero.`,R`They depend only on the connections, not on the element laws.`,R`It holds for any lumped circuit, including L and C.`],
 why:R`The laws are topological; element laws (Ohm’s law, \(v=L\,di/dt\)) are separate.`},
{id:'nodalnat',t:'ana',u:'nodal',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Node analysis',sheet:R`\sum_{\text{branches}}\frac{V-V_k}{R_k}=I_{injected}`,
 q:R`A 12 V source feeds node V through 4 Ω. Node V is connected to ground through 6 Ω and through 12 Ω. Find V.`,
 n:{v:6},
 why:R`\(\frac{V-12}{4}+\frac V6+\frac V{12}=0\). Times 12: \(3V-36+2V+V=0\Rightarrow V=6\) V.`},
{id:'nodalI',t:'ana',u:'nodal',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Node analysis',sheet:R`\sum_{\text{branches}}\frac{V-V_k}{R_k}=I_{injected}`,
 q:R`A 2 A current source injects into node V. Node V connects to ground through 10 Ω and to a 10 V source through 10 Ω. Find V.`,
 n:{v:15},
 why:R`\(\frac{V-10}{10}+\frac{V}{10}=2\Rightarrow2V-10=20\Rightarrow V=15\) V.`},
{id:'supernode',t:'ana',u:'nodal',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Supernode',sheet:R`\text{V-source between two non-reference nodes}\Rightarrow\text{supernode: one KCL + the source constraint}`,
 q:R`When do you form a supernode in node analysis?`,
 o:[R`When a voltage source connects two non-reference nodes`,R`When a current source connects two nodes`,R`When a voltage source connects a node to the reference`,R`When two resistors are in parallel`],a:0,
 why:R`The current through the voltage source is unknown, so write KCL for the two nodes together and add \(V_a-V_b=V_s\). A source to ground simply fixes that node voltage.`},
{id:'nodecount',t:'ana',u:'nodal',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Number of node equations',sheet:R`\text{node equations}=n-1\ (\text{minus one per voltage source})`,
 q:R`A circuit has 5 nodes and no voltage sources. How many independent node equations does node analysis need?`,
 o:[R`4`,R`5`,R`3`,R`6`],a:0,
 why:R`One node is the reference, so \(n-1=4\). Each voltage source removes one more unknown.`},
{id:'meshnat',t:'ana',u:'mesh',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Mesh analysis',sheet:R`\text{mesh } k:\ \sum(\text{own }R)I_k-\sum(\text{shared }R)I_j=\sum V_s`,
 q:R`Mesh 1: a 10 V source, a 2 Ω resistor and a 4 Ω resistor shared with mesh 2. Mesh 2: the shared 4 Ω and another 4 Ω, no source. Find the current in the shared 4 Ω resistor, in A.`,
 n:{v:1.25},
 why:R`\(6I_1-4I_2=10\) and \(-4I_1+8I_2=0\Rightarrow I_1=2I_2\), so \(8I_2=10\): \(I_2=1.25\), \(I_1=2.5\). Shared current \(I_1-I_2=1.25\) A.`},
{id:'supermesh',t:'ana',u:'mesh',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Supermesh',sheet:R`\text{current source shared by two meshes}\Rightarrow\text{supermesh: one KVL + the source constraint}`,
 q:R`When do you form a supermesh in mesh analysis?`,
 o:[R`When a current source is shared by two meshes`,R`When a voltage source is shared by two meshes`,R`When a current source is on the outer boundary of one mesh`,R`When the circuit is non-planar`],a:0,
 why:R`The voltage across the shared current source is unknown, so write KVL round both meshes together and add \(I_1-I_2=I_s\). A source in only one mesh just fixes that mesh current.`},
{id:'meshcount',t:'ana',u:'mesh',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Number of mesh equations',sheet:R`\text{mesh equations}=b-n+1\ (\text{planar circuit})`,
 q:R`A planar circuit has 8 branches and 5 nodes. How many mesh equations are there?`,
 o:[R`4`,R`3`,R`5`,R`8`],a:0,
 why:R`\(b-n+1=8-5+1=4\). Node analysis would need \(n-1=4\) too here.`},
{id:'meshvsnode',t:'ana',u:'mesh',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Choosing mesh or node analysis',sheet:R`\text{pick the method with fewer equations: } n-1\text{ vs } b-n+1`,
 q:R`A circuit has 4 nodes and 9 branches. Which method gives fewer equations?`,
 o:[R`Node analysis (3 equations instead of 6)`,R`Mesh analysis (3 equations instead of 6)`,R`Both give 6`,R`Both give 3`],a:0,
 why:R`Nodes: \(n-1=3\). Meshes: \(b-n+1=6\).`},
{id:'links',t:'ana',u:'topo',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Twigs and links',sheet:R`\text{twigs}=n-1,\ \ \text{links}=b-n+1`,
 q:R`A connected graph has b branches and n nodes. Complete the number of links (independent loops).`,f:R`\text{links}=\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`b-n+1`,R`n-1`,R`b-n`,R`b+n-1`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`A tree uses \(n-1\) branches (twigs); every other branch is a link and closes one fundamental loop.`},
{id:'linksnat',t:'ana',u:'topo',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Twigs and links',sheet:R`\text{twigs}=n-1,\ \ \text{links}=b-n+1`,
 q:R`A network graph has 6 nodes and 10 branches. How many fundamental loops (tie-sets) does it have?`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`Links \(=10-6+1=5\), one fundamental loop each. There are \(n-1=5\) fundamental cut-sets too.`},
{id:'trees',t:'ana',u:'topo',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Number of trees',sheet:R`\text{trees of the complete graph }K_n=n^{\,n-2};\ \text{in general }\det(AA^T)`,
 q:R`How many different trees does the complete graph on 4 nodes have?`,
 n:{v:16},
 why:R`Cayley’s formula \(n^{n-2}=4^2=16\). In general the number of trees is \(\det(A_rA_r^T)\), with \(A_r\) the reduced incidence matrix.`},
{id:'topomsq',t:'ana',u:'topo',lv:2,k:'msq',name:'Incidence matrix, tie-sets and cut-sets',sheet:R`\operatorname{rank}A=n-1;\ \#\text{f-loops}=\#\text{links};\ \#\text{f-cut-sets}=\#\text{twigs}`,
 q:R`For a connected graph with n nodes and b branches, which statements are true?`,
 o:[R`The rank of the complete incidence matrix is \(n-1\)`,R`Each fundamental cut-set contains exactly one twig`,R`Each fundamental loop contains exactly one link`,R`A tree contains b − n + 1 branches`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Its rows sum to zero, so one row is redundant.`,R`It is defined by cutting one twig.`,R`It is defined by adding one link to the tree.`,R`A tree has \(n-1\) branches.`],
 why:R`Twigs define cut-sets, links define loops.`},
{id:'dtoy',t:'ana',u:'ydt',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Delta to star',sheet:R`R_a=\frac{R_{ab}R_{ca}}{R_{ab}+R_{bc}+R_{ca}}`,
 q:R`Complete the star resistance at node a, from a delta \(R_{ab},R_{bc},R_{ca}\).`,f:R`R_a=\frac{\boxed{\;?\;}}{R_{ab}+R_{bc}+R_{ca}}`,
 o:[R`R_{ab}R_{ca}`,R`R_{bc}R_{ca}`,R`R_{ab}R_{bc}`,R`R_{bc}^2`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Use the two delta resistors that touch node a. A balanced delta R becomes a star of R/3.`},
{id:'ytod',t:'ana',u:'ydt',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'Star to delta',sheet:R`R_{ab}=\frac{R_aR_b+R_bR_c+R_cR_a}{R_c}`,
 q:R`Complete the delta resistance between a and b, from a star \(R_a,R_b,R_c\).`,f:R`R_{ab}=\frac{R_aR_b+R_bR_c+R_cR_a}{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`R_c`,R`R_a`,R`R_a+R_b`,R`R_aR_b`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Divide by the star arm opposite the branch. A balanced star R becomes a delta of 3R.`},
{id:'ydbal',t:'ana',u:'ydt',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Star to delta',sheet:R`R_\Delta=3R_Y\ \text{(balanced)}`,
 q:R`A balanced star has 5 Ω in each arm. Find each resistance of the equivalent delta, in Ω.`,
 n:{v:15},
 why:R`\(R_\Delta=3R_Y=15\) Ω, and back again \(R_Y=R_\Delta/3\).`},
{id:'dtoynat',t:'ana',u:'ydt',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Delta to star',sheet:R`R_a=\frac{R_{ab}R_{ca}}{R_{ab}+R_{bc}+R_{ca}}`,
 q:R`A delta has \(R_{ab}=10\) Ω, \(R_{bc}=20\) Ω and \(R_{ca}=30\) Ω. Find the star resistance at node a, in Ω.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`\(\frac{10\times30}{10+20+30}=5\) Ω.`},
{id:'wheat',t:'ana',u:'ydt',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Balanced bridge',sheet:R`\frac{R_1}{R_2}=\frac{R_3}{R_4}\Rightarrow\text{no current in the detector arm}`,
 q:R`In a balanced Wheatstone bridge, the resistor across the middle (the detector arm):`,
 o:[R`Carries no current and can be removed or shorted`,R`Carries the full supply current`,R`Must be converted with a delta–star transformation`,R`Doubles the equivalent resistance`],a:0,
 why:R`Both ends sit at the same potential. Only an unbalanced bridge needs a delta–star step.`},

/* ---------- Network theorems ---------- */
{id:'thevdef',t:'thm',u:'thev',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Thevenin’s theorem',sheet:R`V_{th}=V_{oc},\ \ R_{th}=R_{ab}\text{ with independent sources off}`,
 q:R`The Thevenin voltage of a network at terminals a–b is:`,
 o:[R`The open-circuit voltage across a–b`,R`The voltage across a–b with a short circuit`,R`The sum of the source voltages`,R`The voltage across a–b with the load connected`],a:0,
 why:R`\(V_{th}=V_{oc}\) and \(R_{th}=V_{oc}/I_{sc}\). With only independent sources you can also turn them off and look into a–b.`},
{id:'thevnat',t:'thm',u:'thev',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Thevenin’s theorem',sheet:R`V_{th}=V_{oc},\ \ R_{th}=R_{ab}\text{ with independent sources off}`,
 q:R`A 12 V source is in series with 4 Ω; a 12 Ω resistor is connected across the output terminals a–b. Find \(V_{th}\) at a–b, in V.`,
 n:{v:9},
 why:R`Divider: \(12\times\frac{12}{16}=9\) V. And \(R_{th}=4\parallel12=3\) Ω.`},
{id:'thevload',t:'thm',u:'thev',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Thevenin’s theorem',sheet:R`I_L=\frac{V_{th}}{R_{th}+R_L}`,
 q:R`A 12 V source is in series with 4 Ω; 12 Ω is across a–b. A 6 Ω load is connected at a–b. Find the load current, in A.`,
 n:{v:1},
 why:R`\(V_{th}=9\) V, \(R_{th}=3\) Ω, so \(I_L=9/(3+6)=1\) A.`},
{id:'deact',t:'thm',u:'thev',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Turning sources off',sheet:R`\text{independent }V\to\text{short},\ I\to\text{open};\ \text{dependent sources stay}`,
 q:R`To find \(R_{th}\) by looking into the terminals, independent sources are turned off. How?`,
 o:[R`Voltage sources shorted, current sources opened; dependent sources kept`,R`Voltage sources opened, current sources shorted`,R`All sources shorted, including dependent ones`,R`All sources removed, including dependent ones`],a:0,
 why:R`A zero-volt source is a short; a zero-amp source is an open. Dependent sources depend on circuit variables, so they stay.`},
{id:'nortondef',t:'thm',u:'norton',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Norton’s theorem',sheet:R`I_N=I_{sc}=\frac{V_{th}}{R_{th}},\ \ R_N=R_{th}`,
 q:R`Complete the link between the Thevenin and Norton equivalents.`,f:R`I_N=\boxed{\;?\;},\quad R_N=R_{th}`,
 o:[R`\frac{V_{th}}{R_{th}}`,R`V_{th}R_{th}`,R`\frac{R_{th}}{V_{th}}`,R`V_{th}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Norton’s current is the short-circuit current. It is a source transformation of the Thevenin circuit.`},
{id:'nortonnat',t:'thm',u:'norton',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Norton’s theorem',sheet:R`I_N=I_{sc}`,
 q:R`A 12 V source in series with 4 Ω, and 12 Ω across terminals a–b. Find the Norton current at a–b, in A.`,
 n:{v:3},
 why:R`Shorting a–b shorts the 12 Ω, so \(I_{sc}=12/4=3\) A. Check: \(V_{th}/R_{th}=9/3=3\) A.`},
{id:'nortonrth',t:'thm',u:'norton',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'R_th from V_oc and I_sc',sheet:R`R_{th}=\frac{V_{oc}}{I_{sc}}`,
 q:R`A network with dependent sources gives \(V_{oc}=10\) V and \(I_{sc}=2\) A at its terminals. Its Thevenin resistance is:`,
 o:[R`5 Ω`,R`20 Ω`,R`0.2 Ω`,R`Cannot be found without turning off the dependent sources`],a:0,
 why:R`\(R_{th}=V_{oc}/I_{sc}\) works whatever is inside, which is why it is the standard method with dependent sources.`},
{id:'vociscP',t:'thm',u:'norton',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'R_th from V_oc and I_sc',sheet:R`R_{th}=\frac{V_{oc}}{I_{sc}},\ \ P_L=\left(\frac{V_{oc}}{R_{th}+R_L}\right)^2R_L`,
 q:R`A network has \(V_{oc}=20\) V and \(I_{sc}=4\) A. Find the power in a 5 Ω load, in W.`,
 n:{v:20},
 why:R`\(R_{th}=5\) Ω, so \(I=20/10=2\) A and \(P=4\times5=20\) W (this is also the maximum possible).`},
{id:'superpos',t:'thm',u:'superpos',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Superposition theorem',sheet:R`\text{linear circuit: response}=\sum\text{responses to each independent source alone}`,
 q:R`When does superposition apply?`,
 o:[R`To voltages and currents in a linear circuit with several independent sources`,R`To power in any circuit`,R`To nonlinear circuits with small signals only`,R`Only to DC circuits`],a:0,
 why:R`Power is quadratic in current, so powers don’t superpose. Superposition is also how you handle sources at different frequencies.`},
{id:'superposnat',t:'thm',u:'superpos',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Superposition theorem',sheet:R`V=\sum_k V^{(k)}\ \text{(one independent source at a time)}`,
 q:R`A 10 V source feeds node X through 5 Ω. Node X has 5 Ω to ground, and a 2 A current source also injects into X. Find \(V_X\) in V.`,
 n:{v:10},
 why:R`10 V alone: \(10\times\frac{5}{10}=5\) V. 2 A alone (source shorted): \(2\times(5\parallel5)=5\) V. Total 10 V.`},
{id:'superposP',t:'thm',u:'superpos',lv:2,k:'bug',name:'Superposition and power',sheet:R`P=(I_1+I_2)^2R\ne I_1^2R+I_2^2R`,
 q:R`\(I_1\) and \(I_2\) are the currents in R due to two DC sources acting alone. Find the mistake.`,f:R`P_R=I_1^2R+I_2^2R`,
 o:[R`It should be \((I_1+I_2)^2R\): add the currents first`,R`It should be \((I_1^2+I_2^2)/R\)`,R`It should be \(I_1I_2R\)`,R`Nothing, it is correct`],a:0,
 why:R`The cross term \(2I_1I_2R\) is missing. Powers do add for sources at different frequencies, because the cross term averages to zero.`},
{id:'superdep',t:'thm',u:'superpos',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Superposition with dependent sources',sheet:R`\text{dependent sources stay active in every sub-circuit}`,
 q:R`Applying superposition to a circuit with dependent sources, you should:`,
 o:[R`Turn on one independent source at a time and keep every dependent source active`,R`Treat dependent sources as independent and turn them on one at a time`,R`Remove the dependent sources`,R`Not use superposition at all`],a:0,
 why:R`A dependent source is part of the network, like a resistor. It is not an excitation.`},
{id:'mptdc',t:'thm',u:'mpt',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Maximum power transfer',sheet:R`R_L=R_{th}\Rightarrow P_{max}=\frac{V_{th}^2}{4R_{th}}`,
 q:R`Complete the maximum power delivered to a resistive load.`,f:R`P_{max}=\frac{V_{th}^2}{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`4R_{th}`,R`R_{th}`,R`2R_{th}`,R`8R_{th}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`At \(R_L=R_{th}\) the current is \(V_{th}/2R_{th}\), so \(P=\frac{V_{th}^2}{4R_{th}^2}R_{th}\).`},
{id:'mptac',t:'thm',u:'mpt',lv:2,k:'msq',name:'Maximum power transfer in AC',sheet:R`Z_L\text{ free}: Z_L=Z_{th}^*;\ \ R_L\text{ only}: R_L=|Z_{th}|`,
 q:R`A source has Thevenin impedance \(Z_{th}=R_{th}+jX_{th}\). Which statements are true?`,
 o:[R`If \(Z_L\) is free, maximum power needs \(Z_L=R_{th}-jX_{th}\)`,R`If the load is a pure resistor, maximum power needs \(R_L=|Z_{th}|\)`,R`With \(Z_L=Z_{th}^*\), \(P_{max}=|V_{th}|^2/(4R_{th})\) (rms)`,R`If \(Z_L\) is free, maximum power needs \(Z_L=Z_{th}\)`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`The reactances cancel, leaving a resistive match.`,R`Minimising over \(R_L\) alone gives \(R_L=\sqrt{R_{th}^2+X_{th}^2}\).`,R`Same as the DC result with the reactances cancelled.`,R`That doubles the reactance instead of cancelling it.`],
 why:R`Conjugate match when the load is free; magnitude match when only R can change.`},
{id:'mptnat',t:'thm',u:'mpt',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Maximum power transfer',sheet:R`P_{max}=\frac{V_{th}^2}{4R_{th}}`,
 q:R`A source has \(V_{th}=20\) V and \(R_{th}=5\) Ω. Find the maximum power it can deliver to a resistive load, in W.`,
 n:{v:20},
 why:R`\(\frac{400}{20}=20\) W, with \(R_L=5\) Ω.`},
{id:'mpteff',t:'thm',u:'mpt',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Efficiency at maximum power transfer',sheet:R`\eta=\frac{R_L}{R_L+R_{th}}=50\%\ \text{at } R_L=R_{th}`,
 q:R`At maximum power transfer, the efficiency (load power / power from the Thevenin source) is:`,
 o:[R`50%`,R`100%`,R`25%`,R`75%`],a:0,
 why:R`Half the power is lost in \(R_{th}\). That is why power systems run far from matched conditions.`},
{id:'mptacnat',t:'thm',u:'mpt',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Maximum power transfer in AC',sheet:R`R_L=|Z_{th}|`,
 q:R`\(Z_{th}=3+j4\) Ω. The load is a variable pure resistor. Find the value of \(R_L\) for maximum power, in Ω.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`\(R_L=|Z_{th}|=\sqrt{9+16}=5\) Ω, not 3 Ω.`},

/* ---------- Transient response ---------- */
{id:'tau',t:'tran',u:'rcrl',lv:1,k:'bug',name:'Time constants of RC and RL',sheet:R`\tau_{RC}=RC,\ \ \tau_{RL}=\frac{L}{R}`,
 q:R`Find the mistake.`,f:R`\tau_{RC}=RC,\qquad\tau_{RL}=\frac{R}{L}`,
 o:[R`The RL time constant is \(L/R\)`,R`The RC time constant is \(R/C\)`,R`The RL time constant is \(LR\)`,R`Nothing, it is correct`],a:0,
 why:R`Check units: henry/ohm = second. A bigger inductance makes the circuit slower, so L goes on top.`},
{id:'rcnat',t:'tran',u:'rcrl',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'RC charging',sheet:R`v_C(t)=V\left(1-e^{-t/RC}\right)`,
 q:R`An uncharged 1 mF capacitor charges from 10 V through 1 kΩ. Find \(v_C\) at t = 1 s, to 2 decimals.`,
 n:{v:6.32121,tol:0.006},
 why:R`\(\tau=10^3\times10^{-3}=1\) s. \(v_C=10(1-e^{-1})\approx6.32\) V: 63.2% after one time constant.`},
{id:'fivetau',t:'tran',u:'rcrl',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Settling after a few time constants',sheet:R`1-e^{-t/\tau}:\ 63.2\%\ (1\tau),\ 86.5\%\ (2\tau),\ 99.3\%\ (5\tau)`,
 q:R`After how many time constants is a first-order response within 1% of its final value?`,
 o:[R`About 5`,R`1`,R`2`,R`10`],a:0,
 why:R`\(e^{-5}\approx0.0067\), so after \(5\tau\) the response is 99.3% of the way there.`},
{id:'rlnat',t:'tran',u:'rcrl',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'RL current rise',sheet:R`i(t)=\frac VR\left(1-e^{-Rt/L}\right)`,
 q:R`A 12 V source is switched onto a series 4 Ω, 2 H circuit at t = 0. Find the current at t = 0.5 s, to 3 decimals.`,
 n:{v:1.89636,tol:0.002},
 why:R`\(\tau=L/R=0.5\) s and \(i(\infty)=3\) A, so \(i=3(1-e^{-1})\approx1.896\) A.`},
{id:'firstorder',t:'tran',u:'rcrl',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'General first-order response',sheet:R`x(t)=x(\infty)+\left[x(0^+)-x(\infty)\right]e^{-t/\tau}`,
 q:R`Complete the response of any first-order circuit with a DC source.`,f:R`x(t)=x(\infty)+\left[\boxed{\;?\;}-x(\infty)\right]e^{-t/\tau}`,
 o:[R`x(0^+)`,R`x(0^-)`,R`0`,R`x(\tau)`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Only three numbers are needed: initial value, final value, time constant. Use \(0^+\) values; for \(v_C\) and \(i_L\) they equal the \(0^-\) values.`},
{id:'icap0',t:'tran',u:'init',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Initial current into an uncharged capacitor',sheet:R`v_C(0^+)=0\Rightarrow C\text{ acts as a short at }0^+`,
 q:R`An uncharged capacitor in series with 5 Ω is switched onto 10 V at t = 0. Find the current at \(t=0^+\), in A.`,
 n:{v:2},
 why:R`The capacitor voltage cannot jump, so it is 0 at \(0^+\): all 10 V is across 5 Ω, giving 2 A.`},
{id:'ind0',t:'tran',u:'init',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'An inductor at the switching instant',sheet:R`i_L(0^+)=0\Rightarrow L\text{ acts as an open circuit at }0^+`,
 q:R`A series R–L circuit (zero initial current) is switched onto V at t = 0. At \(t=0^+\) the inductor voltage is:`,
 o:[R`V`,R`0`,R`V/2`,R`Infinite`],a:0,
 why:R`The current is still 0, so there is no drop across R and the inductor takes the whole source voltage.`},
{id:'finalLC',t:'tran',u:'init',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'L and C at DC steady state',sheet:R`t\to\infty\text{ (DC)}: C\to\text{open},\ L\to\text{short}`,
 q:R`A DC source drives a network of R, L and C. To find the final values (\(t\to\infty\)), replace:`,
 o:[R`C by an open circuit and L by a short circuit`,R`C by a short circuit and L by an open circuit`,R`Both by short circuits`,R`Both by open circuits`],a:0,
 why:R`Final steady state is DC: nothing changes, so no capacitor current and no inductor voltage. At \(0^+\) it is the reverse for uncharged elements.`},
{id:'didt0',t:'tran',u:'init',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Initial slope di/dt',sheet:R`v_L(0^+)=L\frac{di}{dt}(0^+)`,
 q:R`A 10 V source is switched onto a series 5 Ω, 2 H circuit (zero initial current). Find \(di/dt\) at \(t=0^+\), in A/s.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`At \(0^+\) the current is 0, so \(v_L=10\) V and \(di/dt=10/2=5\) A/s.`},
{id:'dvdtnat',t:'tran',u:'init',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Capacitor charged by a constant current',sheet:R`\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{i}{C}`,
 q:R`A 4 mA constant current charges a 2 µF capacitor. Find \(dv/dt\) in V/s.`,
 n:{v:2000},
 why:R`\(\frac{4\times10^{-3}}{2\times10^{-6}}=2000\) V/s. The voltage is a ramp.`},
{id:'rlcdamp',t:'tran',u:'rlc2',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Damping of a series RLC circuit',sheet:R`\text{series: }\alpha=\frac{R}{2L},\ \omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}},\ \zeta=\frac R2\sqrt{\frac CL}`,
 q:R`Complete the neper frequency of a series RLC circuit.`,f:R`s^2+2\alpha s+\omega_0^2=0,\quad\alpha=\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`\frac{R}{2L}`,R`\frac{1}{2RC}`,R`\frac{R}{L}`,R`\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(1/(2RC)\) is the parallel-RLC value. In series, more R means more damping; in parallel, more R means less.`},
{id:'critR',t:'tran',u:'rlc2',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Critical damping resistance',sheet:R`\text{series RLC critically damped: }R=2\sqrt{L/C}`,
 q:R`A series RLC circuit has L = 0.1 H and C = 10 µF. Find R for critical damping, in Ω.`,
 n:{v:200},
 why:R`\(\sqrt{L/C}=\sqrt{10^4}=100\), so \(R=200\) Ω. Larger R: overdamped; smaller: underdamped (oscillatory).`},
{id:'dampcase',t:'tran',u:'rlc2',lv:1,k:'cond',name:'Overdamped, critical, underdamped',sheet:R`\alpha>\omega_0:\text{over};\ \alpha=\omega_0:\text{critical};\ \alpha\lt\omega_0:\text{under}`,
 q:R`When is the natural response of an RLC circuit oscillatory?`,
 o:[R`When \(\alpha\lt\omega_0\) (complex roots)`,R`When \(\alpha>\omega_0\)`,R`When \(\alpha=\omega_0\)`,R`When R = 0 only`],a:0,
 why:R`Roots \(-\alpha\pm\sqrt{\alpha^2-\omega_0^2}\) are complex when \(\alpha\lt\omega_0\), giving a decaying sinusoid at \(\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}\).`},
{id:'parrlc',t:'tran',u:'rlc2',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Damping of a parallel RLC circuit',sheet:R`\text{parallel: }\alpha=\frac{1}{2RC}`,
 q:R`In a parallel RLC circuit, increasing R:`,
 o:[R`Reduces damping (the response rings longer)`,R`Increases damping`,R`Does not change damping`,R`Changes \(\omega_0\)`],a:0,
 why:R`\(\alpha=1/(2RC)\) falls as R rises; an open R (infinite) means no damping at all. \(\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}\) does not involve R.`},
{id:'wdnat',t:'tran',u:'rlc2',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Damped natural frequency',sheet:R`\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\alpha^2}`,
 q:R`A series RLC circuit has R = 4 Ω, L = 1 H and C = 1/8 F. Find the damped natural frequency \(\omega_d\), in rad/s.`,
 n:{v:2},
 why:R`\(s^2+4s+8=0\Rightarrow s=-2\pm j2\). So \(\alpha=2\), \(\omega_0=\sqrt8\), and \(\omega_d=2\) rad/s.`},
{id:'sdomL',t:'tran',u:'sdom',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'s-domain model of an inductor',sheet:R`L\to sL\ \text{in series with a source } L\,i(0^-)`,
 q:R`An inductor with initial current \(i(0^-)\) becomes, in the s-domain, an impedance sL in series with a voltage source of:`,f:R`\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`L\,i(0^-)`,R`\frac{i(0^-)}{s}`,R`sL\,i(0^-)`,R`\frac{L}{i(0^-)}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`From \(\mathcal L\{L\,di/dt\}=sLI(s)-L\,i(0^-)\). The parallel form uses a current source \(i(0^-)/s\).`},
{id:'sdomC',t:'tran',u:'sdom',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'s-domain model of a capacitor',sheet:R`C\to\frac{1}{sC}\ \text{in series with a source }\frac{v(0^-)}{s}`,
 q:R`A capacitor with initial voltage \(v(0^-)\) becomes an impedance \(1/sC\) in series with a voltage source of:`,f:R`\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`\frac{v(0^-)}{s}`,R`C\,v(0^-)`,R`s\,v(0^-)`,R`\frac{v(0^-)}{sC}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`A constant initial voltage is a step, whose transform is \(v(0^-)/s\). The parallel form uses a current source \(C\,v(0^-)\).`},
{id:'fvtcond',t:'tran',u:'sdom',lv:2,k:'cond',name:'Final value theorem',sheet:R`f(\infty)=\lim_{s\to0}sF(s)\ \ \text{if all poles of } sF(s)\text{ are in the open left half-plane}`,
 q:R`When can you use \(f(\infty)=\lim_{s\to0}sF(s)\)?`,
 o:[R`When every pole of \(sF(s)\) has a negative real part`,R`Always`,R`When F(s) has no zeros`,R`When F(s) is a proper fraction`],a:0,
 why:R`For \(F=\frac{1}{s^2+1}\) the formula gives 0, but \(\sin t\) has no final value. Check the poles first.`},
{id:'ivtfvt',t:'tran',u:'sdom',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Final value theorem',sheet:R`f(0^+)=\lim_{s\to\infty}sF(s),\ \ f(\infty)=\lim_{s\to0}sF(s)`,
 q:R`A current has \(I(s)=\frac{2s+3}{s(s+1)}\). Find its final value \(i(\infty)\), in A.`,
 n:{v:3},
 why:R`\(sI(s)=\frac{2s+3}{s+1}\to3\) as \(s\to0\) (the pole at −1 is stable). The initial value is \(\lim_{s\to\infty}=2\).`},
{id:'rcpole',t:'tran',u:'sdom',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Pole of an RC circuit',sheet:R`\frac{V_C(s)}{V_{in}(s)}=\frac{1}{1+sRC},\ \ \text{pole } s=-\frac1{RC}`,
 q:R`A series RC circuit has R = 2 Ω and C = 0.5 F. Find the pole of \(V_C(s)/V_{in}(s)\).`,
 n:{v:-1},
 why:R`\(\frac{1}{1+sRC}=\frac{1}{1+s}\), so the pole is at \(s=-1/RC=-1\). It sets the time constant 1 s.`},
{id:'actrcond',t:'tran',u:'actr',lv:2,k:'cond',name:'Switching an AC source onto RL',sheet:R`v=V_m\sin(\omega t+\theta)\text{ on RL}:\ i_{tr}\propto\sin(\theta-\phi)e^{-Rt/L},\ \phi=\tan^{-1}\frac{\omega L}{R}`,
 q:R`\(v=V_m\sin(\omega t+\theta)\) is switched onto a series RL circuit at t = 0 (zero initial current). When is there no transient?`,
 o:[R`When \(\theta=\tan^{-1}(\omega L/R)\)`,R`When \(\theta=0\)`,R`When \(\theta=90^\circ\)`,R`Never: an RL circuit always has a transient`],a:0,
 why:R`The transient term is proportional to \(\sin(\theta-\phi)\). Switching when \(\theta-\phi=\pm90^\circ\) gives the largest DC offset.`},
{id:'actrnat',t:'tran',u:'actr',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Switching an AC source onto RL',sheet:R`\text{no transient when }\theta=\phi=\tan^{-1}\frac{\omega L}{R}`,
 q:R`A series RL circuit has R = 10 Ω and \(\omega L=10\) Ω. At what switching angle θ (in degrees, \(0\le\theta\lt180\)) of \(v=V_m\sin(\omega t+\theta)\) is there no transient?`,
 n:{v:45},
 why:R`\(\phi=\tan^{-1}(10/10)=45^\circ\). Switch when \(\theta=\phi\).`},
{id:'doubling',t:'tran',u:'actr',lv:3,k:'mcq',name:'Doubling effect',sheet:R`\theta-\phi=\pm90^\circ,\ \omega L\gg R:\ i_{peak}\to2I_m`,
 q:R`A nearly pure inductor (\(\omega L\gg R\)) is switched onto an AC source at the worst instant. The first current peak approaches:`,
 o:[R`About twice the steady-state peak`,R`The steady-state peak`,R`Half the steady-state peak`,R`Infinity`],a:0,
 why:R`A slowly decaying DC offset of size \(I_m\) adds to the AC current. This is the doubling effect in transformer and fault currents.`},
{id:'actrmsq',t:'tran',u:'actr',lv:2,k:'msq',name:'Switching an AC source onto RL',sheet:R`i(t)=I_m\sin(\omega t+\theta-\phi)-I_m\sin(\theta-\phi)e^{-t/\tau}`,
 q:R`An AC source is switched onto a series RL circuit at t = 0 with zero initial current. Which statements are true?`,
 o:[R`The current is a steady-state sinusoid plus a decaying exponential`,R`The exponential decays with time constant L/R`,R`The exponential term makes \(i(0^+)=0\)`,R`The exponential term oscillates at the source frequency`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Forced response plus natural response.`,R`The natural response of RL has \(\tau=L/R\).`,R`It cancels the steady-state value at t = 0, because the inductor current cannot jump.`,R`It is a pure exponential (a DC offset that dies out).`],
 why:R`The size of the offset depends on the switching instant.`},

/* ---------- Sinusoidal steady state ---------- */
{id:'zlc',t:'ac',u:'phasor',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Impedance of L and C',sheet:R`Z_L=j\omega L,\ \ Z_C=\frac{1}{j\omega C}=-\frac{j}{\omega C}`,
 q:R`Complete the capacitor impedance.`,f:R`Z_C=\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`-\frac{j}{\omega C}`,R`\frac{j}{\omega C}`,R`-j\omega C`,R`j\omega C`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(\frac1{j}=-j\). A capacitor’s reactance is negative and falls with frequency; an inductor’s is positive and rises.`},
{id:'leadlag',t:'ac',u:'phasor',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Phase of current in L and C',sheet:R`L:\ I\text{ lags }V\text{ by }90^\circ;\ \ C:\ I\text{ leads }V\text{ by }90^\circ\ (\text{“CIVIL”})`,
 q:R`In a pure capacitor, the current:`,
 o:[R`Leads the voltage by 90°`,R`Lags the voltage by 90°`,R`Is in phase with the voltage`,R`Leads the voltage by 45°`],a:0,
 why:R`“CIVIL”: in C, I comes before V; in L, V comes before I.`},
{id:'znat',t:'ac',u:'phasor',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Impedance magnitude',sheet:R`|Z|=\sqrt{R^2+X^2},\ \ I=\frac{V}{|Z|}`,
 q:R`A series circuit has R = 3 Ω and \(X_L=4\) Ω on a 100 V (rms) supply. Find the rms current, in A.`,
 n:{v:20},
 why:R`\(|Z|=5\) Ω, so \(I=100/5=20\) A, lagging by \(\tan^{-1}(4/3)=53.13^\circ\).`},
{id:'admbug',t:'ac',u:'phasor',lv:2,k:'bug',name:'Admittance of a series RX branch',sheet:R`Y=\frac{1}{R+jX}=\frac{R}{R^2+X^2}-j\frac{X}{R^2+X^2}`,
 q:R`A branch has impedance \(Z=R+jX\). Find the mistake.`,f:R`Y=\frac1Z=\frac1R-\frac{j}{X}`,
 o:[R`\(Y=\frac{R-jX}{R^2+X^2}\): you can’t invert term by term`,R`It should be \(\frac1R+\frac jX\)`,R`It should be \(R-jX\)`,R`Nothing, it is correct`],a:0,
 why:R`Term-by-term inversion works only for elements in parallel. For a series branch, multiply by the conjugate.`},
{id:'zrc',t:'ac',u:'phasor',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Impedance magnitude',sheet:R`|Z|=\sqrt{R^2+X^2}`,
 q:R`A series circuit has R = 8 Ω and \(X_C=6\) Ω. Find |Z| in Ω.`,
 n:{v:10},
 why:R`\(\sqrt{64+36}=10\) Ω; the current leads by \(\tan^{-1}(6/8)=36.87^\circ\).`},
{id:'rmssine',t:'ac',u:'rms',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'RMS of a sinusoid',sheet:R`V_{rms}=\frac{V_m}{\sqrt2},\ \ V_{avg}\ (\text{half cycle})=\frac{2V_m}{\pi}`,
 q:R`Complete the rms value of \(V_m\sin\omega t\).`,f:R`V_{rms}=\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`\frac{V_m}{\sqrt2}`,R`\frac{2V_m}{\pi}`,R`\frac{V_m}{2}`,R`\frac{V_m}{\sqrt3}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(2V_m/\pi\) is the half-cycle average; \(V_m/\sqrt3\) is the rms of a triangle wave.`},
{id:'rmscomb',t:'ac',u:'rms',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'RMS of DC plus harmonics',sheet:R`V_{rms}=\sqrt{V_0^2+\sum_kV_{k,rms}^2}`,
 q:R`Find the rms value of \(v=10+10\sqrt2\sin\omega t\), to 2 decimals.`,
 n:{v:14.14214,tol:0.006},
 why:R`\(\sqrt{10^2+10^2}=14.14\). Components at different frequencies add in squares, not directly.`},
{id:'rmshw',t:'ac',u:'rms',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'RMS of a half-wave rectified sine',sheet:R`\text{half-wave: }V_{avg}=\frac{V_m}{\pi},\ V_{rms}=\frac{V_m}{2}`,
 q:R`Find the rms value of a half-wave rectified sine of peak 10 V.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`Half the cycles are zero, so the mean square halves: \(V_{rms}=\sqrt{\frac{V_m^2}{4}}=\frac{V_m}{2}=5\) V. The average is \(10/\pi\approx3.18\) V.`},
{id:'rmsshapes',t:'ac',u:'rms',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'RMS of standard waveforms',sheet:R`\text{square: }V_m;\ \text{sine: }\frac{V_m}{\sqrt2};\ \text{triangle/sawtooth: }\frac{V_m}{\sqrt3}`,
 q:R`Each waveform has peak value \(V_m\) and zero average. Which rms values are correct?`,
 o:[R`Square wave (±\(V_m\)): \(V_m\)`,R`Triangle wave: \(V_m/\sqrt3\)`,R`Sine wave: \(V_m/\sqrt2\)`,R`Triangle wave: \(V_m/\sqrt2\)`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`\(v^2=V_m^2\) at every instant.`,R`The mean of \(t^2\) over a linear ramp gives 1/3.`,R`The mean of \(\sin^2\) is 1/2.`,R`That is the sine value.`],
 why:R`The flatter the wave, the higher the rms for the same peak.`},
{id:'formfactor',t:'ac',u:'rms',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Form and crest factor',sheet:R`\text{sine: FF}=\frac{V_{rms}}{V_{avg}}=1.11,\ \ \text{CF}=\frac{V_m}{V_{rms}}=1.414`,
 q:R`The form factor (rms / average over a half cycle) of a sine wave is:`,
 o:[R`1.11`,R`1.414`,R`0.637`,R`1`],a:0,
 why:R`\(\frac{V_m/\sqrt2}{2V_m/\pi}=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt2}\approx1.11\). The crest factor is \(\sqrt2\approx1.414\).`},
{id:'acdiv',t:'ac',u:'acana',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'AC voltage divider',sheet:R`V_2=V_s\frac{Z_2}{Z_1+Z_2}`,
 q:R`A 10 V (rms) source drives a 3 Ω resistor in series with a 4 Ω inductive reactance. Find the rms voltage across the inductor, in V.`,
 n:{v:8},
 why:R`\(|V_L|=10\times\frac{4}{|3+j4|}=8\) V. The resistor has 6 V, and \(6+8\ne10\) because the phasors are 90° apart.`},
{id:'acsum',t:'ac',u:'acana',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Phasor addition of voltages',sheet:R`V_s=\sqrt{V_R^2+(V_L-V_C)^2}`,
 q:R`In a series RLC circuit the voltmeter readings are \(V_R=30\) V, \(V_L=80\) V and \(V_C=40\) V. Find the supply voltage, in V.`,
 n:{v:50},
 why:R`\(\sqrt{30^2+(80-40)^2}=50\) V. Adding readings arithmetically (150 V) is the trap.`},
{id:'acpar',t:'ac',u:'acana',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Phasor addition of currents',sheet:R`I=\sqrt{I_R^2+(I_C-I_L)^2}`,
 q:R`A resistor and a capacitor in parallel draw 3 A and 4 A (rms). Find the supply current, in A.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`The capacitor current leads the resistor current by 90°: \(\sqrt{9+16}=5\) A.`},
{id:'multifreq',t:'ac',u:'acana',lv:2,k:'cond',name:'Phasors with sources of different frequencies',sheet:R`\text{different frequencies: solve each with phasors, add in the time domain}`,
 q:R`A circuit has two sources at different frequencies. How do you find a branch current?`,
 o:[R`Solve for each source separately with its own impedances, then add the time functions`,R`Add the two source phasors and solve once`,R`Use the average frequency for all impedances`,R`Phasor methods cannot be used at all`],a:0,
 why:R`A phasor carries one frequency only. Reactances differ at each frequency, so use superposition.`},

/* ---------- Resonance ---------- */
{id:'f0',t:'res',u:'sres',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Resonant frequency',sheet:R`\omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}},\ \ f_0=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}`,
 q:R`Complete the resonant frequency of a series RLC circuit.`,f:R`f_0=\frac{1}{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`2\pi\sqrt{LC}`,R`\sqrt{LC}`,R`2\pi LC`,R`2\pi\sqrt{L/C}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`At \(\omega_0\), \(\omega L=1/\omega C\). Forgetting \(2\pi\) gives \(\omega_0\) in rad/s, not \(f_0\) in Hz.`},
{id:'sresmsq',t:'res',u:'sres',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'Series resonance',sheet:R`\text{series resonance: }Z=R\ (\min),\ I\ \max,\ \text{pf}=1,\ V_L=V_C=QV`,
 q:R`Which statements hold at series resonance?`,
 o:[R`The impedance is minimum and equal to R`,R`The power factor is unity`,R`The capacitor voltage can be much larger than the supply voltage`,R`The current is minimum`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`The reactances cancel.`,R`Current and voltage are in phase.`,R`\(V_C=QV\), and Q can be large.`,R`The current is maximum, \(V/R\).`],
 why:R`Series resonance is voltage magnification; parallel resonance is current magnification.`},
{id:'w0nat',t:'res',u:'sres',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Resonant frequency',sheet:R`\omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}`,
 q:R`L = 10 mH and C = 1 µF. Find \(\omega_0\) in rad/s.`,
 n:{v:10000},
 why:R`\(LC=10^{-8}\), so \(\omega_0=10^4\) rad/s (\(f_0\approx1592\) Hz).`},
{id:'vcnat',t:'res',u:'sres',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Voltage magnification',sheet:R`V_L=V_C=Q\,V\ \text{at series resonance}`,
 q:R`A series RLC circuit with Q = 10 is driven at resonance by 5 V (rms). Find the rms capacitor voltage, in V.`,
 n:{v:50},
 why:R`\(V_C=IX_C=\frac{V}{R}\cdot\frac{1}{\omega_0C}=QV=50\) V.`},
{id:'presmcq',t:'res',u:'pres',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Parallel resonance',sheet:R`\text{parallel (tank) resonance: } Z\ \max,\ \text{line current}\ \min`,
 q:R`At the resonant frequency of a parallel LC tank circuit:`,
 o:[R`The impedance is maximum and the line current is minimum`,R`The impedance is minimum and the line current is maximum`,R`The impedance is zero`,R`The branch currents are zero`],a:0,
 why:R`The inductor and capacitor currents are large but cancel each other, so little current flows from the supply.`},
{id:'presRL',t:'res',u:'pres',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Resonance of a practical tank circuit',sheet:R`(R+j\omega L)\parallel C:\ \omega_0=\sqrt{\frac{1}{LC}-\frac{R^2}{L^2}}`,
 q:R`A coil (R = 3 Ω, L = 1 H) is in parallel with C = 0.04 F. Find the resonant frequency (unity power factor) in rad/s.`,
 n:{v:4},
 why:R`\(\sqrt{\frac{1}{0.04}-\frac{9}{1}}=\sqrt{25-9}=4\) rad/s. The coil resistance pulls it below \(1/\sqrt{LC}=5\).`},
{id:'zdyn',t:'res',u:'pres',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'Dynamic impedance of a tank circuit',sheet:R`Z_{dyn}=\frac{L}{CR}`,
 q:R`A coil (R, L) is in parallel with C. Complete the impedance at resonance.`,f:R`Z_{dyn}=\frac{\boxed{\;?\;}}{CR}`,
 o:[R`L`,R`1`,R`R^2`,R`\sqrt{L}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`It is purely resistive and grows as the coil resistance R falls. Check: L/(CR) has the unit of ohms.`},
{id:'qseries',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Q of a series RLC circuit',sheet:R`Q_s=\frac{\omega_0L}{R}=\frac{1}{R}\sqrt{\frac LC}`,
 q:R`Complete the quality factor of a series RLC circuit.`,f:R`Q=\frac{1}{R}\sqrt{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`\frac LC`,R`\frac CL`,R`LC`,R`\frac{1}{LC}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(\omega_0L/R\) with \(\omega_0=1/\sqrt{LC}\). For parallel RLC it inverts: \(Q_p=R\sqrt{C/L}\).`},
{id:'bwseries',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Bandwidth and Q',sheet:R`\text{BW}=\omega_2-\omega_1=\frac{\omega_0}{Q}=\frac{R}{L}\ \text{(series)}`,
 q:R`Complete the bandwidth of a series RLC circuit, in rad/s.`,f:R`\text{BW}=\frac{\omega_0}{\boxed{\;?\;}}=\frac{R}{L}`,
 o:[R`Q`,R`Q^2`,R`\sqrt Q`,R`2Q`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Higher Q means sharper selectivity and a narrower band.`},
{id:'qnat',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Q of a series RLC circuit',sheet:R`Q_s=\frac{\omega_0L}{R}`,
 q:R`A series circuit has R = 10 Ω, L = 0.1 H and C = 10 µF. Find Q.`,
 n:{v:10},
 why:R`\(\omega_0=1/\sqrt{10^{-6}}=1000\) rad/s, so \(Q=1000\times0.1/10=10\).`},
{id:'bwnat',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Bandwidth and Q',sheet:R`\text{BW}=\frac{f_0}{Q}`,
 q:R`A tuned circuit has \(f_0=1\) MHz and Q = 50. Find its bandwidth in kHz.`,
 n:{v:20},
 why:R`\(10^6/50=20\,000\) Hz = 20 kHz.`},
{id:'halfpower',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:2,k:'msq',name:'Half-power frequencies',sheet:R`\omega_1\omega_2=\omega_0^2,\ \ I(\omega_{1,2})=\frac{I_{max}}{\sqrt2}`,
 q:R`For a series RLC circuit, which statements about the half-power frequencies \(\omega_1\lt\omega_0\lt\omega_2\) are true?`,
 o:[R`The current there is \(I_{max}/\sqrt2\)`,R`\(\omega_0=\sqrt{\omega_1\omega_2}\)`,R`The impedance angle there is ±45°`,R`\(\omega_0=(\omega_1+\omega_2)/2\) exactly`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Power \(\propto I^2\) halves.`,R`The response is geometrically symmetric about \(\omega_0\).`,R`There \(|X|=R\).`,R`That is only approximately true for high Q.`],
 why:R`Geometric mean, not arithmetic mean.`},
{id:'qpar',t:'res',u:'qbw',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Q of a parallel RLC circuit',sheet:R`Q_p=\omega_0RC=R\sqrt{\frac CL}`,
 q:R`In a parallel RLC circuit, increasing the resistance R:`,
 o:[R`Increases Q and narrows the bandwidth`,R`Decreases Q and widens the bandwidth`,R`Does not change Q`,R`Changes the resonant frequency`],a:0,
 why:R`\(Q_p=R\sqrt{C/L}\): R is in the numerator, the opposite of the series case. An ideal tank (R infinite) has infinite Q.`},

/* ---------- Complex power & power factor ---------- */
{id:'sconj',t:'pow',u:'cpow',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Complex power',sheet:R`S=V I^*=P+jQ\ \ (\text{rms phasors})`,
 q:R`Complete the complex power (rms phasors).`,f:R`S=P+jQ=V\cdot\boxed{\;?\;}`,
 o:[R`I^*`,R`I`,R`\frac{I}{2}`,R`|I|`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`The conjugate makes the angle \(\theta_V-\theta_I\), so an inductive load (current lagging) gives positive Q.`},
{id:'snat',t:'pow',u:'cpow',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Complex power',sheet:R`P=|V||I|\cos(\theta_V-\theta_I)`,
 q:R`\(V=100\angle30^\circ\) V and \(I=5\angle-15^\circ\) A (rms). Find the real power, in W, to 1 decimal.`,
 n:{v:353.553,tol:0.06},
 why:R`\(S=500\angle45^\circ\), so \(P=500\cos45^\circ\approx353.6\) W and \(Q\approx353.6\) var (inductive).`},
{id:'qsign',t:'pow',u:'cpow',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Sign of reactive power',sheet:R`\text{inductive load: }Q>0\text{ (absorbs)};\ \text{capacitor: }Q\lt0\text{ (supplies)}`,
 q:R`With \(S=VI^*\), a capacitor:`,
 o:[R`Absorbs negative reactive power (it supplies vars)`,R`Absorbs positive reactive power`,R`Absorbs real power`,R`Has zero apparent power`],a:0,
 why:R`Current leads voltage, so \(\theta_V-\theta_I=-90^\circ\) and \(Q\lt0\). That is why capacitors correct lagging power factor.`},
{id:'striangle',t:'pow',u:'cpow',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Power triangle',sheet:R`S=\sqrt{P^2+Q^2},\ \ \text{pf}=\frac PS`,
 q:R`A load takes 300 W and 400 var. Find its power factor.`,
 n:{v:0.6},
 why:R`\(S=500\) VA and pf \(=300/500=0.6\) (lagging, since Q > 0).`},
{id:'pmsq',t:'pow',u:'cpow',lv:2,k:'msq',name:'Complex power facts',sheet:R`\sum S_{\text{sources}}=\sum S_{\text{loads}};\ P_L=P_C=0`,
 q:R`Which statements are true in sinusoidal steady state?`,
 o:[R`The average power taken by an ideal inductor is zero`,R`Total complex power supplied equals the total complex power absorbed`,R`Real power is consumed only in resistances`,R`Apparent powers of loads always add arithmetically`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`It stores and returns energy each cycle.`,R`Conservation of complex power (Tellegen).`,R`L and C have zero average power.`,R`S adds as complex numbers; magnitudes add only if the angles match.`],
 why:R`P and Q are each conserved separately; |S| is not.`},
{id:'pfdef',t:'pow',u:'pf',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Power factor',sheet:R`\text{pf}=\frac{P}{|S|}=\cos(\theta_V-\theta_I)\ \text{(sinusoids)}`,
 q:R`Complete the definition of power factor.`,f:R`\text{pf}=\frac{P}{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`|S|`,R`Q`,R`P+Q`,R`V`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`For sinusoids it equals \(\cos\phi\). With harmonics it is also reduced by distortion (see Power Electronics).`},
{id:'qcfill',t:'pow',u:'pf',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'Power factor correction',sheet:R`Q_C=P(\tan\phi_1-\tan\phi_2)`,
 q:R`A load of real power P is corrected from angle \(\phi_1\) to \(\phi_2\). Complete the capacitor kvar.`,f:R`Q_C=P\left(\boxed{\;?\;}-\tan\phi_2\right)`,
 o:[R`\tan\phi_1`,R`\cos\phi_1`,R`\sin\phi_1`,R`\cot\phi_1`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`P stays the same; only the reactive power changes, from \(P\tan\phi_1\) to \(P\tan\phi_2\).`},
{id:'pfcnat',t:'pow',u:'pf',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Power factor correction',sheet:R`Q_C=P(\tan\phi_1-\tan\phi_2)`,
 q:R`A 10 kW load runs at 0.6 pf lagging. Find the capacitor kvar needed to raise the pf to 1, to 2 decimals.`,
 n:{v:13.3333,tol:0.006},
 why:R`\(\tan\phi_1=0.8/0.6=4/3\), so \(Q_C=10\times\frac43\approx13.33\) kvar.`},
{id:'cuF',t:'pow',u:'pf',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Capacitance for a given kvar',sheet:R`Q_C=\frac{V^2}{X_C}=V^2\omega C`,
 q:R`A capacitor must supply 1000 var at 230 V, 50 Hz. Find C in µF, to 1 decimal.`,
 n:{v:60.17,tol:0.06},
 why:R`\(C=\frac{Q}{V^2\omega}=\frac{1000}{230^2\times314.16}\approx60.2\) µF.`},
{id:'pfwhy',t:'pow',u:'pf',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Why correct power factor',sheet:R`\text{same }P,\ \text{higher pf}\Rightarrow\text{lower }I\Rightarrow\text{lower }I^2R\text{ losses}`,
 q:R`A shunt capacitor raises a load’s power factor. For the same real power, it:`,
 o:[R`Reduces the line current and the line losses`,R`Reduces the real power taken by the load`,R`Increases the line current`,R`Changes the load’s own current`],a:0,
 why:R`The capacitor supplies the vars locally, so the line carries less reactive current. The load itself draws the same current as before.`},

/* ---------- Two-port networks ---------- */
{id:'zdef',t:'tp',u:'zy',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Z parameters',sheet:R`z_{11}=\frac{V_1}{I_1}\Big|_{I_2=0},\ z_{21}=\frac{V_2}{I_1}\Big|_{I_2=0}`,
 q:R`Complete the definition of \(z_{11}\).`,f:R`z_{11}=\frac{V_1}{I_1}\Big|_{\boxed{\;?\;}}`,
 o:[R`I_2=0`,R`V_2=0`,R`I_1=0`,R`V_1=0`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`Z parameters are open-circuit parameters (the other port’s current is zero). Y parameters are short-circuit ones (\(V_2=0\)).`},
{id:'ydef',t:'tp',u:'zy',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Y parameters',sheet:R`y_{11}=\frac{I_1}{V_1}\Big|_{V_2=0},\ \ Y=Z^{-1}`,
 q:R`Y parameters are also called:`,
 o:[R`Short-circuit admittance parameters`,R`Open-circuit impedance parameters`,R`Hybrid parameters`,R`Transmission parameters`],a:0,
 why:R`Each is measured with the other port shorted. And \(Y=Z^{-1}\) when both exist.`},
{id:'ztee',t:'tp',u:'zy',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Z parameters of a T network',sheet:R`\text{T: } z_{11}=Z_a+Z_c,\ z_{12}=z_{21}=Z_c,\ z_{22}=Z_b+Z_c`,
 q:R`A T network has series arms 2 Ω (port 1 side) and 3 Ω (port 2 side), and a 4 Ω shunt arm. Find \(z_{22}\) in Ω.`,
 n:{v:7},
 why:R`With port 1 open, port 2 sees 3 + 4 = 7 Ω. Also \(z_{11}=6\) and \(z_{12}=z_{21}=4\) Ω.`},
{id:'ypi',t:'tp',u:'zy',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Y parameters of a π network',sheet:R`\pi:\ y_{11}=Y_a+Y_c,\ y_{12}=y_{21}=-Y_c,\ y_{22}=Y_b+Y_c`,
 q:R`A π network has shunt admittances 1 S (port 1) and 2 S (port 2) and a series admittance 3 S. Find \(y_{12}\) in S.`,
 n:{v:-3},
 why:R`Short port 1 and drive port 2: \(I_1=-3V_2\), so \(y_{12}=-3\) S. Also \(y_{11}=4\) and \(y_{22}=5\) S.`},
{id:'ztoy',t:'tp',u:'zy',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Converting Z to Y',sheet:R`Y=Z^{-1}:\ y_{11}=\frac{z_{22}}{\Delta z}`,
 q:R`A two-port has \(z_{11}=z_{22}=4\) Ω and \(z_{12}=z_{21}=2\) Ω. Find \(y_{11}\) in S, to 3 decimals.`,
 n:{v:0.33333,tol:0.002},
 why:R`\(\Delta z=16-4=12\), so \(y_{11}=z_{22}/\Delta z=4/12\approx0.333\) S, not \(1/z_{11}=0.25\).`},
{id:'abcd',t:'tp',u:'habcd',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'ABCD parameters',sheet:R`V_1=AV_2+BI_2,\ \ I_1=CV_2+DI_2\ \ (I_2\text{ leaving port 2})`,
 q:R`Complete the transmission (ABCD) equations, with \(I_2\) leaving port 2.`,f:R`V_1=AV_2+B\,I_2,\qquad I_1=\boxed{\;?\;}\,V_2+D\,I_2`,
 o:[R`C`,R`A`,R`B`,R`D`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`A and D are dimensionless, B is in ohms and C in siemens. For a reciprocal network \(AD-BC=1\).`},
{id:'hunits',t:'tp',u:'habcd',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'h parameters',sheet:R`V_1=h_{11}I_1+h_{12}V_2,\ \ I_2=h_{21}I_1+h_{22}V_2`,
 q:R`Which statements about h parameters are true?`,
 o:[R`\(h_{11}\) is an impedance (ohms)`,R`\(h_{22}\) is an admittance (siemens)`,R`\(h_{12}\) and \(h_{21}\) are dimensionless`,R`All four are measured with port 2 open`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`\(V_1/I_1\) with \(V_2=0\).`,R`\(I_2/V_2\) with \(I_1=0\).`,R`Voltage ratio and current ratio.`,R`\(h_{11},h_{21}\) need port 2 shorted; \(h_{12},h_{22}\) need port 1 open.`],
 why:R`“Hybrid” because the units are mixed. They are the natural choice for transistors.`},
{id:'abcdser',t:'tp',u:'habcd',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'ABCD of a series impedance',sheet:R`\text{series } Z:\ \begin{bmatrix}1&Z\\0&1\end{bmatrix};\ \ \text{shunt } Y:\ \begin{bmatrix}1&0\\Y&1\end{bmatrix}`,
 q:R`A two-port is a single series 5 Ω resistor. Find its B parameter, in Ω.`,
 n:{v:5},
 why:R`\(V_1=V_2+5I_2\) and \(I_1=I_2\), so A = 1, B = 5, C = 0, D = 1.`},
{id:'abcdD',t:'tp',u:'habcd',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'ABCD parameters',sheet:R`\text{reciprocal: }AD-BC=1`,
 q:R`A reciprocal two-port has A = 2, B = 3 Ω and C = 1 S. Find D.`,
 n:{v:2},
 why:R`\(2D-3=1\Rightarrow D=2\). Since A = D, the network is also symmetric.`},
{id:'recip',t:'tp',u:'tpconv',lv:1,k:'msq',name:'Reciprocity conditions',sheet:R`\text{reciprocal: }z_{12}=z_{21},\ y_{12}=y_{21},\ AD-BC=1,\ h_{12}=-h_{21}`,
 q:R`Which conditions mean a two-port is reciprocal?`,
 o:[R`\(z_{12}=z_{21}\)`,R`\(AD-BC=1\)`,R`\(h_{12}=-h_{21}\)`,R`\(A=D\)`],a:[0,1,2],
 ex:[R`Swapping source and meter gives the same transfer impedance.`,R`The transmission matrix has determinant 1.`,R`Note the minus sign for h.`,R`That is the symmetry condition.`],
 why:R`Any network of R, L, C and coupled coils is reciprocal; dependent sources usually break it.`},
{id:'symm',t:'tp',u:'tpconv',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Symmetry conditions',sheet:R`\text{symmetric: }z_{11}=z_{22},\ y_{11}=y_{22},\ A=D,\ \Delta h=1`,
 q:R`A two-port is symmetric (the ports can be swapped) when:`,
 o:[R`\(A=D\)`,R`\(B=C\)`,R`\(AD-BC=1\)`,R`\(A=-D\)`],a:0,
 why:R`Equivalently \(z_{11}=z_{22}\). \(AD-BC=1\) is reciprocity, a different property.`},
{id:'interconn',t:'tp',u:'tpconv',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Interconnecting two-ports',sheet:R`\text{series: add }Z;\ \text{parallel: add }Y;\ \text{cascade: multiply ABCD}`,
 q:R`Two two-ports are connected in cascade. The overall parameters are found by:`,
 o:[R`Multiplying their ABCD matrices in order`,R`Adding their Z matrices`,R`Adding their Y matrices`,R`Adding their h matrices`],a:0,
 why:R`Series–series adds Z, parallel–parallel adds Y, series–parallel adds h. Cascade multiplies transmission matrices; the order matters.`},
{id:'zinload',t:'tp',u:'tpconv',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Input impedance of a loaded two-port',sheet:R`Z_{in}=\frac{AZ_L+B}{CZ_L+D}`,
 q:R`A two-port has A = 2, B = 3 Ω, C = 1 S, D = 2. Port 2 is terminated in 1 Ω. Find the input impedance, in Ω, to 3 decimals.`,
 n:{v:1.66667,tol:0.002},
 why:R`\(Z_{in}=\frac{2(1)+3}{1(1)+2}=\frac53\approx1.667\) Ω.`},

/* ---------- Balanced three-phase circuits ---------- */
{id:'starVL',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Line and phase values: star',sheet:R`\text{star: }V_L=\sqrt3V_{ph},\ I_L=I_{ph};\ \ \text{delta: }V_L=V_{ph},\ I_L=\sqrt3I_{ph}`,
 q:R`Complete the star connection relation.`,f:R`V_L=\boxed{\;?\;}\,V_{ph},\qquad I_L=I_{ph}`,
 o:[R`\sqrt3`,R`1`,R`3`,R`\frac{1}{\sqrt3}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`A line voltage is the phasor difference of two phase voltages 120° apart, which gives \(\sqrt3\) times the magnitude.`},
{id:'deltaIL',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Line and phase values: delta',sheet:R`\text{delta: }V_L=V_{ph},\ I_L=\sqrt3I_{ph}`,
 q:R`In a balanced delta load, the line current is:`,
 o:[R`\(\sqrt3\) times the phase current`,R`Equal to the phase current`,R`\(1/\sqrt3\) times the phase current`,R`3 times the phase current`],a:0,
 why:R`Each line current is the difference of two phase currents. The voltage across each phase equals the line voltage.`},
{id:'starnat',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Line and phase values: star',sheet:R`V_{ph}=\frac{V_L}{\sqrt3}`,
 q:R`A balanced star load of 10 Ω (resistive) per phase is on a 400 V (line) supply. Find the line current, in A, to 2 decimals.`,
 n:{v:23.094,tol:0.006},
 why:R`\(V_{ph}=400/\sqrt3=230.9\) V, so \(I=23.09\) A, equal in line and phase for star.`},
{id:'phaseshift',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Phase of line voltage in star',sheet:R`\text{positive sequence: }V_{AB}=\sqrt3V_{AN}\angle30^\circ`,
 q:R`In a positive-sequence (ABC) star system, the line voltage \(V_{AB}\):`,
 o:[R`Leads \(V_{AN}\) by 30°`,R`Lags \(V_{AN}\) by 30°`,R`Is in phase with \(V_{AN}\)`,R`Leads \(V_{AN}\) by 120°`],a:0,
 why:R`\(V_{AB}=V_{AN}-V_{BN}\); with \(V_{BN}\) at −120°, the difference sits at +30°. For negative sequence it lags.`},
{id:'neutral',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:1,k:'mcq',name:'Neutral current in a balanced star',sheet:R`\text{balanced: }I_a+I_b+I_c=0\Rightarrow I_N=0`,
 q:R`In a balanced star-connected load, the neutral current is:`,
 o:[R`Zero`,R`Equal to one line current`,R`\(\sqrt3\) times a line current`,R`Three times a line current`],a:0,
 why:R`Three equal phasors 120° apart sum to zero. The neutral wire matters only for unbalanced loads (and triplen harmonics).`},
{id:'dtoyeq',t:'tph',u:'bal',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Delta load as an equivalent star',sheet:R`Z_Y=\frac{Z_\Delta}{3}`,
 q:R`A balanced delta load has 30 Ω per phase. For per-phase (star) analysis, what impedance per phase do you use, in Ω?`,
 n:{v:10},
 why:R`\(Z_Y=Z_\Delta/3=10\) Ω, used with the line-to-neutral voltage.`},
{id:'p3fill',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:1,k:'fill',name:'Three-phase power',sheet:R`P=\sqrt3V_LI_L\cos\phi=3V_{ph}I_{ph}\cos\phi`,
 q:R`Complete the total power of a balanced three-phase load.`,f:R`P=\boxed{\;?\;}\,V_LI_L\cos\phi`,
 o:[R`\sqrt3`,R`3`,R`1`,R`\frac{1}{\sqrt3}`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`It holds for both star and delta, with \(\phi\) the angle of the phase impedance (not between line quantities).`},
{id:'p3nat',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:1,k:'nat',name:'Three-phase power',sheet:R`P=\sqrt3V_LI_L\cos\phi`,
 q:R`A balanced load draws 10 A from a 400 V (line) supply at 0.8 pf. Find the power in kW, to 2 decimals.`,
 n:{v:5.54256,tol:0.006},
 why:R`\(\sqrt3\times400\times10\times0.8\approx5543\) W.`},
{id:'twowatt',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:2,k:'fill',name:'Two-wattmeter method',sheet:R`P=W_1+W_2,\ \ \tan\phi=\sqrt3\,\frac{W_1-W_2}{W_1+W_2}`,
 q:R`Complete the power factor angle from the two wattmeter readings.`,f:R`\tan\phi=\boxed{\;?\;}\,\frac{W_1-W_2}{W_1+W_2}`,
 o:[R`\sqrt3`,R`3`,R`\frac{1}{\sqrt3}`,R`1`],om:1,a:0,
 why:R`\(W_1-W_2=V_LI_L\sin\phi\) while \(W_1+W_2=\sqrt3V_LI_L\cos\phi\).`},
{id:'twowattnat',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Two-wattmeter method',sheet:R`\tan\phi=\sqrt3\,\frac{W_1-W_2}{W_1+W_2}`,
 q:R`Two wattmeters read 2 kW and 1 kW on a balanced load. Find the power factor, to 3 decimals.`,
 n:{v:0.86603,tol:0.002},
 why:R`\(\tan\phi=\sqrt3\times\frac13=\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\), so \(\phi=30^\circ\) and pf = 0.866.`},
{id:'wattzero',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:2,k:'mcq',name:'Two-wattmeter method',sheet:R`W_{1,2}=V_LI_L\cos(30^\circ\mp\phi)`,
 q:R`In the two-wattmeter method on a balanced load, one wattmeter reads zero. The power factor is:`,
 o:[R`0.5`,R`0`,R`0.866`,R`1`],a:0,
 why:R`\(W_2=V_LI_L\cos(30^\circ+\phi)=0\) at \(\phi=60^\circ\). Below pf 0.5 that meter reads negative; at unity pf both read equal.`},
{id:'deltavsstar',t:'tph',u:'p3',lv:2,k:'nat',name:'Star vs delta connection of the same impedances',sheet:R`P_\Delta=3P_Y\ \text{(same impedances, same line voltage)}`,
 q:R`Three equal impedances take 2 kW when star-connected to a supply. How many kW do they take when delta-connected to the same supply?`,
 n:{v:6},
 why:R`In delta each impedance gets \(\sqrt3\) times the voltage, so 3 times the power: 6 kW.`}
]);
})();
